---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import RangeBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/RangeBuild.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="range-tick-marks">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;input type="range"&gt;</code> handles dragging, keyboard steps and
      the form value. Styling it means vendor pseudo-elements for the track and the
      thumb, and only Firefox has one for the filled part.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tick marks have a native answer too: point <code>list</code> at a <code
        >&lt;datalist&gt;</code
      >. The datalist itself stays hidden, so the labels never show.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>appearance: none</code> on the input and on the thumb, then style
        {" "}<code>::-webkit-slider-runnable-track</code> and <code
          >::-moz-range-track</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        Firefox paints the fill with <code>::-moz-range-progress</code>.
        Elsewhere a one-color <code>linear-gradient</code> sized to <code
          >--track-fill</code
        > does it
      </li>
      <li>
        The thumb's position sets the fill, no JavaScript. With <code
          >overflow: hidden</code
        > the input is a scroller, so the thumb can be a <code
          >view-timeline</code
        >. <code>timeline-scope</code> lets the input animate on it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--track-fill</code> is registered with <code>@property</code>, so
        keyframes can animate it. The timeline runs from the end edge, so they
        go from <code>100%</code> to <code>0%</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Padding and an equal negative margin leave room for the thumb and its
        halo without moving anything. <code>view-timeline-inset</code> takes it back
        out of the timeline, <code>outline-offset</code> out of the focus ring
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>display: flex</code> brings the datalist back, and <code
          >justify-content: space-between</code
        > spreads its options
      </li>
      <li>
        Zero-width options with centered labels sit on each step. Half a thumb
        of padding lines the ends up with the thumb's center
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`@property --track-fill {
  syntax: "<percentage>";
  inherits: true;
  initial-value: 0%;
}

@keyframes range-fill {
  from {
    --track-fill: 100%;
  }

  to {
    --track-fill: 0%;
  }
}

@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
  input[type="range"] {
    animation-fill-mode: both;
    animation-name: range-fill;
    animation-range: contain;
    animation-timeline: --thumb;
    animation-timing-function: linear;
    box-sizing: content-box;
    margin: -0.75rem;
    outline-offset: -0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    timeline-scope: --thumb;
  }

  input[type="range"]:dir(rtl) {
    animation-direction: reverse;
  }

  input[type="range"]::-webkit-slider-thumb {
    view-timeline: --thumb inline;
    view-timeline-inset: 0.75rem;
  }
}

input[type="range"]::-webkit-slider-runnable-track {
  background-color: var(--track-color);
  background-image: linear-gradient(var(--fill-color), var(--fill-color));
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size: var(--track-fill, 0%) 100%;
}

input[type="range"]:dir(rtl)::-webkit-slider-runnable-track {
  background-position: right;
}

datalist {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding-inline: calc(3ex / 2);
}

datalist > option {
  display: flex;
  inline-size: 0;
  justify-content: center;
  white-space: nowrap;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <RangeBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without scroll-driven animations <code>--track-fill</code> stays at <code
          >0%</code
        >: no fill, the slider still works. <code
          >@supports (animation-timeline: view())</code
        > keeps the padding and <code>overflow: hidden</code> out too. Firefox fills
        natively
      </li>
      <li>
        The labels are plain flex content and render wherever <code
          >&lt;datalist&gt;</code
        > is parsed
      </li>
      <li>
        In right-to-left, <code>:dir(rtl)</code> moves the gradient to the right edge
        and reverses the animation
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
